<div class="prose">

<div class="panel">
  <div class="panel-body">
    <span class="eyebrow">Learn</span>
    <h1 style="font-size:26px;letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:6px 0 12px">
      The things nobody sat you down and explained</h1>
    <div class="lead">
      The calculator gives you a number. This page explains every idea behind it —
      what each account actually does, why your tax bracket is not what you lose on
      the next dollar, and the unglamorous groundwork (an emergency fund, expensive
      debt) that comes <b>before</b> any of it. Written for people who have never
      been told how this works, not for the financially fluent.
    </div>
    <div class="toc">
      <a href="#order">Order of operations</a>
      <a href="#accounts">The accounts</a>
      <a href="#room">Contribution room</a>
      <a href="#investing">What goes inside</a>
      <a href="#stockrisk">Individual stocks</a>
      <a href="#marginal">Your real marginal rate</a>
      <a href="#credits">Credits &amp; deductions</a>
      <a href="#pensions">CPP, OAS &amp; GIS</a>
      <a href="#budget">Budgeting</a>
      <a href="#emergency">Emergency fund</a>
      <a href="#debt">Debt</a>
      <a href="#growth">Compound growth</a>
      <a href="#dollars">Future dollars</a>
      <a href="#limits">What this does not model</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ORDER -->
<div class="panel sec" id="order">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Order of operations</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Almost every question of the form <em>"should I put money in X or Y?"</em> has the
    same answer, and it is an order rather than a single account:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>1. Cover your minimum debt payments.</b> Missing them costs more than any
      account earns, in interest and in credit damage.</li>
      <li><b>2. Build a small starter emergency fund.</b> Without one, the first
      unexpected bill goes on a credit card and undoes everything else.</li>
      <li><b>3. Take the full employer match.</b> A 50% match is an immediate 50%
      return. Nothing else on this page competes with that.</li>
      <li><b>4. Clear high-interest debt.</b> Paying off a card charging 20% is a
      guaranteed 20% return. No investment offers a guaranteed 20%.</li>
      <li><b>5. Finish the emergency fund</b> — three to six months of essential
      spending.</li>
      <li><b>6. Fill registered accounts</b> in whatever order your own marginal rate
      justifies. That is the part the calculator works out for you.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Why the calculator can skip steps 1–5</span>
      It assumes you have already handled them, because it cannot see your debts or
      your cash cushion. If you have a credit card balance, the honest answer is that
      paying it off beats every allocation the calculator will suggest.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ACCOUNTS -->
<div class="panel sec" id="accounts">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">The registered accounts</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>"Registered" means registered with the Canada Revenue Agency for special tax
    treatment. None of them is an investment. Each is a <b>container</b> you put
    investments inside, and the container decides how the money is taxed. A TFSA
    holding only cash is the most common and most expensive misunderstanding in
    Canadian personal finance.</p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="lt">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>Account</th><th>Tax going in</th><th>Tax coming out</th><th>{{PAGE_YEAR}} limit</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>TFSA</b></td><td>No deduction</td><td>Nothing, ever</td>
              <td class="n">{{$accounts.tfsa.annual_limit}}/yr</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>RRSP</b></td><td>Deductible</td><td>Taxed as income</td>
              <td class="n">{{%accounts.rrsp.earned_income_rate}} of earned income,<br>max {{$accounts.rrsp.dollar_limit}}</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>FHSA</b></td><td>Deductible</td><td>Nothing, for a first home</td>
              <td class="n">{{$accounts.fhsa.annual_limit}}/yr,<br>{{$accounts.fhsa.lifetime_limit}} lifetime</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>RESP</b></td><td>No deduction</td><td>Taxed to the student</td>
              <td class="n">{{$accounts.resp.lifetime_contribution}} lifetime<br>per child</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Non-registered</b></td><td>No deduction</td><td>Tax on gains and income</td>
              <td class="n">No limit</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>TFSA — Tax-Free Savings Account</h3>
    <p>Money goes in after tax; everything it earns is yours permanently. You can
    withdraw any amount, any time, for any reason, and the room comes back — but only
    on <b>January 1 of the following year</b>, which is where people accidentally
    over-contribute. Withdrawals do not count as income, so they never reduce
    income-tested benefits. That makes the TFSA the most flexible account in the
    system and the right default when you are unsure.</p>
    <div class="keyfacts">
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">{{PAGE_YEAR}} room</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.tfsa.annual_limit}}</div></div>
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Total since 2009</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.tfsa.cumulative_since_2009}}</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>That cumulative figure applies if you were 18 or older in 2009 and have never
    contributed. If you turned 18 later, your room starts accumulating from that year.</p>

    <h3>RRSP — Registered Retirement Savings Plan</h3>
    <p>A contribution is a <b>deduction</b>: it comes off your taxable income, so you
    get back your marginal rate on every dollar. You pay tax later, when you withdraw.
    The RRSP is therefore a bet that your rate in retirement will be lower than your
    rate today — and when it is not, the account actively loses you money.</p>
    <p>At 71 an RRSP must be converted, usually to a RRIF, which forces a minimum
    withdrawal every year whether you want the income or not. Two withdrawal
    programmes let you take money out early without tax: the Home Buyers' Plan
    (up to <span class="num">{{$accounts.hbp_withdrawal_limit}}</span> toward a first
    home, repayable) and the Lifelong Learning Plan for education.</p>
    <div class="callout warnbox">
      <span class="t">Where an RRSP backfires</span>
      On a modest income, RRSP and RRIF withdrawals in retirement count as income for
      the Guaranteed Income Supplement, which claws back roughly 50 cents on the
      dollar. Someone who contributed at a 20% marginal rate can face an effective
      50%+ rate on the way out. The calculator refuses to recommend an RRSP in that
      situation, and says so.
    </div>

    <h3>FHSA — First Home Savings Account</h3>
    <p>The only account that is deductible going in <b>and</b> tax-free coming out,
    provided the money buys a qualifying first home. It is strictly better than an
    RRSP for anyone eligible, and badly under-used.</p>
    <div class="keyfacts">
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Per year</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.fhsa.annual_limit}}</div></div>
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Lifetime</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.fhsa.lifetime_limit}}</div></div>
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Max in one year</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.fhsa.max_single_year_with_carryforward}}</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>Unused room carries forward one year, which is why a single year can take
    {{$accounts.fhsa.max_single_year_with_carryforward}}. The account has a limited
    life: it must be closed by the end of the 15th year after opening, or the year you
    turn 71, or the year after your first qualifying withdrawal — whichever comes
    first. If you never buy a home, the balance can be rolled into an RRSP without
    using RRSP room, so opening one early costs you very little.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Why the projection changes after a few years</span>
      At {{$accounts.fhsa.annual_limit}} a year the lifetime cap is reached in five
      full years. The calculator's chart shows government help stepping down when that
      happens, rather than pretending the deduction continues to retirement.
    </div>

    <h3>RESP — Registered Education Savings Plan</h3>
    <p>For a child's post-secondary education. Contributions are not deductible, but
    the government adds a grant — the Canada Education Savings Grant — worth
    <span class="num">{{%accounts.resp.cesg_match_rate}}</span> of what you put in.</p>
    <div class="keyfacts">
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Grant rate</div><div class="v">{{%accounts.resp.cesg_match_rate}}</div></div>
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Grant per year</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.resp.cesg_annual_max}}</div></div>
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Grant lifetime</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.resp.cesg_lifetime_max}}</div></div>
      <div class="kf"><div class="k">Contribution lifetime</div><div class="v">{{$accounts.resp.lifetime_contribution}}</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>Contributing {{$accounts.resp.cesg_matched_contribution}} in a year captures the
    full {{$accounts.resp.cesg_annual_max}} grant. Beyond that the grant stops, though
    contributions still grow tax-sheltered. Withdrawals of growth and grant are taxed
    in the <b>student's</b> hands, which usually means little or no tax. Lower-income
    families may also qualify for the Canada Learning Bond, which pays into an RESP
    without any contribution at all.</p>

    <h3>Employer match and workplace pensions</h3>
    <p>If your employer contributes when you do, that is an immediate return no
    investment can match — a 50-cent match is 50% before the money is even invested.
    Take all of it first.</p>
    <div class="callout warnbox">
      <span class="t">The match is not free of limits</span>
      A group RRSP match uses the <b>same</b> contribution room your own deposits use,
      and both the employee and employer portions count. A defined-contribution pension
      instead generates a pension adjustment that reduces next year's RRSP room.
      Either way, room is consumed. Contributing past your limit is penalised at
      <b>1% per month</b> on the excess above a {{PAGE_RRSP_CUSHION}} lifetime cushion,
      and the CRA charges the employee, not the employer.
    </div>

    <h3>Non-registered ("taxable") accounts</h3>
    <p>Once registered room is full, an ordinary investment account is still worth
    using — you simply pay tax on what it earns, and different income types are taxed
    differently. Capital gains are only partly taxable and only when you sell, eligible
    Canadian dividends receive a credit, and interest is taxed at your full rate. That
    ranking is why interest-paying investments are usually the first thing to shelter
    inside a TFSA or RRSP.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ROOM -->
<div class="panel sec" id="room">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Contribution room</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Every registered account has a limit, and the limits work differently:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>RRSP room</b> is earned. Each year you accumulate
      {{%accounts.rrsp.earned_income_rate}} of your previous year's earned income, up
      to {{$accounts.rrsp.dollar_limit}}, less any pension adjustment. Unused room
      carries forward indefinitely.</li>
      <li><b>TFSA room</b> is granted. Everyone 18 or older gets the same
      {{$accounts.tfsa.annual_limit}} regardless of income, it carries forward
      indefinitely, and withdrawals are restored the following calendar year.</li>
      <li><b>FHSA room</b> starts only when you <em>open</em> the account, which is why
      opening one early matters even if you cannot fund it yet.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Check before you contribute</span>
      Your real numbers are in <b>CRA My Account</b>, and they are the only ones that
      count. The calculator estimates RRSP room from the income you type, which ignores
      carried-forward room and any pension adjustment — it can easily be out by
      thousands in either direction.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- INVESTING -->
<div class="panel sec" id="investing">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">What goes inside the accounts</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>A TFSA is not an investment and neither is an RRSP. They are containers, and
    they grow at whatever you put inside them. Choosing the container is a tax
    decision; choosing the contents is an investment decision. The calculator handles
    the first and assumes the second.</p>

    <h3>The main things you can hold</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cash and GICs.</b> Predictable, principal protected, and over long periods
      they have historically struggled to beat inflation. Right for money you need
      within a few years — an emergency fund, a down payment eighteen months out.</li>
      <li><b>Bonds.</b> Lending to a government or a company for interest. Steadier than
      stocks, lower expected return, and they lose value when interest rates rise.</li>
      <li><b>Stocks (equities).</b> Part-ownership of businesses. The highest expected
      long-run return and the highest short-run volatility. Falls of 30–50% have
      happened repeatedly and will happen again.</li>
      <li><b>Everything else</b> — real estate, commodities, crypto — carries its own
      risks and, in the case of crypto, no cash flow and no long history to reason
      from.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Risk is not one thing</h3>
    <p>Two different ideas share the word. <b>Volatility</b> is how much a price moves
    around; it is uncomfortable but it recovers. <b>Permanent loss</b> is capital that
    does not come back — a single company failing, or selling at the bottom and never
    buying back in. Broad diversification does very little about the first and a great
    deal about the second.</p>
    <p>Your time horizon decides how much volatility you can carry. Money needed next
    year does not belong in stocks. Money for a retirement three decades away has time
    to sit through several full market cycles.</p>

    <h3>Index funds and ETFs</h3>
    <p>An <b>index fund</b> holds every company in a market in proportion to size,
    rather than trying to pick winners. An <b>ETF</b> — exchange-traded fund — is a
    fund that trades on an exchange like a stock; most of the widely held ones are
    index funds. A single broad ETF can hold thousands of companies across dozens of
    countries, which is diversification you could not assemble yourself at any
    reasonable cost.</p>
    <p><b>Asset-allocation ETFs</b> go one step further: a single fund holding a fixed
    mix of global stocks and bonds, rebalanced for you. One purchase, one ongoing
    decision, no spreadsheet. For most people the realistic alternative is not a
    better portfolio — it is no portfolio.</p>

    <h3>Fees compound exactly like returns</h3>
    <p>A fund's <b>MER</b> (management expense ratio) is charged every year on your
    whole balance, whether the fund gains or loses. It sounds trivial and is not.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">{{FEE_MONTHLY}} a month for {{FEE_YEARS}} years at {{FEE_GROSS}} before fees</span>
      At a <b>{{MER_LOW}}</b> MER you would finish with <b>{{FEE_LOW}}</b>.
      At <b>{{MER_HIGH}}</b> — ordinary for an actively managed Canadian mutual fund —
      you would finish with <b>{{FEE_HIGH}}</b>.
      The difference is <b>{{FEE_GAP}}</b>, on {{FEE_CONTRIB}} of contributions.
      The fee gap is larger than a third of everything you put in.
      <em>Computed with the same compounding the calculator uses.</em>
    </div>
    <p>Canada has historically had some of the highest mutual fund fees in the
    developed world. Ask what you are paying — the MER, plus any advice or trading fee
    on top. It is the one variable here you control completely.</p>

    <h3>Diversification, and the home-country trap</h3>
    <p>Canada is a small slice of the world's stock market — a low single-digit
    percentage — and it is heavily concentrated in financials and energy. A portfolio
    of only Canadian stocks is a bet on two sectors of one economy. Holding global
    equities is not exotic; it is the default that Canadian-only is a deviation
    from.</p>

    <h3>Asset location: which account holds what</h3>
    <p>Once you hold more than one account, <em>where</em> an investment sits changes
    its after-tax return.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The United States withholds tax on dividends paid to foreign investors. Under
      the Canada–US treaty an <b>RRSP</b> is exempt for US-listed US equities; a
      <b>TFSA</b> is not, and the withholding cannot be recovered.</li>
      <li>Interest is taxed at your full marginal rate, so interest-paying investments
      benefit most from being sheltered.</li>
      <li>Capital gains and eligible Canadian dividends are taxed more lightly, so they
      are the most tolerable things to hold in a non-registered account.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Worth knowing, not worth contorting for</span>
      These rules are specific and depend on how a fund is listed and structured.
      Getting the contribution made matters far more than getting the location perfect.
      Check the details before restructuring anything.
    </div>

    <h3>Boring habits that do most of the work</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Automate the contribution</b> so it does not depend on how you feel that
      month.</li>
      <li><b>Rebalance occasionally</b> — once a year is plenty — back to your intended
      mix.</li>
      <li><b>Do not try to time the market.</b> Missing a handful of the best days,
      which cluster inside the worst periods, does lasting damage to a long-run
      return.</li>
      <li><b>Expect declines and plan not to sell into them.</b> The largest avoidable
      risk in a long-horizon portfolio is the investor's own behaviour in a bad
      year.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- STOCK RISK -->
<div class="panel sec" id="stockrisk">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">The risks of picking individual stocks</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Buying shares in individual companies is the version of investing that gets
    talked about, and it carries risks that a broad fund does not.</p>

    <h3>Company-specific risk pays you nothing</h3>
    <p>Risk splits into two parts. <b>Market risk</b> is the whole market moving; you
    cannot diversify it away, and bearing it is what long-run returns compensate you
    for. <b>Company-specific risk</b> is one firm losing a lawsuit, a patent, or a
    chief executive. It <em>can</em> be diversified away almost for free — so the
    market does not pay you a premium for carrying it. Holding five stocks instead of
    five thousand adds risk without adding expected return.</p>

    <h3>Most stocks are not the market</h3>
    <p>Long-run studies of stock returns keep finding the same shape: a small minority
    of companies account for nearly all of the market's gain over decades, while the
    typical stock underperforms and a large share lose money outright. An index owns
    the winners automatically. A concentrated portfolio has to find them, and the base
    rate for doing so is unfavourable.</p>
    <p>This is also why hindsight is so misleading. Every enormous winner is obvious
    afterwards, and the companies that looked equally promising and failed are not the
    ones anybody writes about.</p>

    <div class="callout warnbox">
      <span class="t">Employer shares are a double exposure</span>
      If your savings are concentrated in your employer's stock, your income and your
      portfolio depend on one company. The bad scenario takes both at once, which is
      exactly when you can least afford it. Discounted share-purchase plans can still
      be worth taking — selling and diversifying afterwards is usually the point.
    </div>

    <h3>Who is on the other side of your trade</h3>
    <p>Every time you buy, somebody with a view is selling. Often that somebody is a
    full-time institution with faster data and lower costs. That does not make picking
    stocks impossible, but it should set expectations: you are competing, not simply
    participating.</p>

    <h3>Speculation is a separate category</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Leverage and margin</b> magnify losses as well as gains and can force a
      sale at the worst possible moment.</li>
      <li><b>Options</b> can expire worthless — a total loss on that position is a
      normal outcome, not a freak one.</li>
      <li><b>Crypto and meme stocks</b> have no earnings to anchor a valuation; prices
      rest on what the next buyer will pay.</li>
      <li><b>Anything promoted to you</b> — a hot tip, a message from a stranger, a
      guaranteed return — deserves the assumption that you are the exit. Check
      registration through the CSA's National Registration Search before sending money
      anywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout warnbox">
      <span class="t">Two Canadian traps</span>
      <b>Frequent trading inside a TFSA</b> can be treated by the CRA as carrying on a
      business, making the profits fully taxable — the shelter is not unconditional.
      And <b>capital losses in a TFSA or RRSP cannot be claimed</b> against gains
      elsewhere, so a losing position in a registered account loses twice.
    </div>

    <h3>If you want to do it anyway</h3>
    <p>Plenty of people find individual stocks genuinely interesting, and interest is
    worth something if it keeps you engaged with your own finances. The common
    compromise is to keep the long-term plan in broad, low-cost funds and to size
    individual positions as a small, deliberately capped share of the total — an amount
    you could lose entirely without changing your retirement.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Where the calculator stands</span>
      It assumes a single steady rate of return and takes no view on what you hold. It
      cannot tell you whether your portfolio is diversified, what you are paying in
      fees, or whether an 8% assumption is reasonable for your mix. Those are yours to
      check.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- MARGINAL -->
<div class="panel sec" id="marginal">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Your real marginal rate</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Canada taxes income in brackets, and only the income inside each bracket is
    taxed at that bracket's rate. Earning one dollar into a higher bracket never
    reduces your take-home pay — the "I got a raise and lost money" story is almost
    always about something else on this page.</p>
    <p>Your <b>marginal rate</b> is what you lose on the next dollar. Your posted
    bracket is only part of it, because income-tested benefits shrink as income rises,
    and a shrinking benefit is indistinguishable from a tax.</p>

    <h3>The Canada Child Benefit</h3>
    <p>The CCB pays up to {{$benefits.ccb.max_under_6}} a year for a child under 6 and
    {{$benefits.ccb.max_6_to_17}} for a child aged 6 to 17. It is reduced once adjusted
    family net income passes {{$benefits.ccb.threshold_1}}, at a rate that depends on
    how many children you have, then at a lower rate above
    {{$benefits.ccb.threshold_2}}.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="lt">
        <thead><tr><th>Children</th>
          <th>Reduction above {{$benefits.ccb.threshold_1}}</th>
          <th>Reduction above {{$benefits.ccb.threshold_2}}</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase1_rates.1}}</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase2_rates.1}}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase1_rates.2}}</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase2_rates.2}}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase1_rates.3}}</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase2_rates.3}}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4 or more</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase1_rates.4}}</td><td class="n">{{%benefits.ccb.phase2_rates.4}}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Those percentages stack on top of income tax. A three-child family in the first
    band loses {{%benefits.ccb.phase1_rates.3}} of every extra dollar in benefit
    <em>plus</em> their income tax — which is how a household whose posted bracket says
    28.2% actually keeps barely half of a raise.</p>

    <h3>The BC Family Benefit</h3>
    <p>A provincial benefit on top of the CCB: up to
    {{$benefits.bc_family_benefit.max_first_child}} for a first child,
    {{$benefits.bc_family_benefit.max_second_child}} for a second and
    {{$benefits.bc_family_benefit.max_additional_child}} for each additional one, plus
    up to {{$benefits.bc_family_benefit.single_parent_supplement}} for a single parent.
    It reduces at {{%benefits.bc_family_benefit.phaseout_rate}} of AFNI above
    {{$benefits.bc_family_benefit.threshold_1}} — but only down to a floor
    ({{$benefits.bc_family_benefit.floor_first_child}} for the first child). The floor
    then holds, costing nothing at the margin, until
    {{$benefits.bc_family_benefit.threshold_2}}, above which it reduces at
    {{%benefits.bc_family_benefit.phaseout_rate}} again.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Why the shape matters</span>
      That is two clawback bands with a flat plateau between them. Your BCFB marginal
      cost is {{%benefits.bc_family_benefit.phaseout_rate}}, then nothing, then
      {{%benefits.bc_family_benefit.phaseout_rate}} again. A raise that feels identical
      can cost very different amounts depending on which band you are in.
    </div>

    <h3>The Canada Groceries and Essentials Benefit</h3>
    <p>The renamed GST/HST credit: up to {{$benefits.cgeb.max_single}} for a single
    person, {{$benefits.cgeb.max_couple}} for a couple, plus
    {{$benefits.cgeb.max_per_child}} per child. Note the age limit is
    <b>under {{PAGE_CGEB_AGE}}</b>, not the CCB's under-18. It reduces at
    {{%benefits.cgeb.phaseout_rate}} of AFNI above {{$benefits.cgeb.threshold}}, so it
    adds {{%benefits.cgeb.phaseout_rate}} to the effective marginal rate across
    that band.</p>
    <div class="callout warnbox">
      <span class="t">Not independently verified</span>
      The BCFB and CGEB figures on this page were supplied to the project rather than
      taken from a CRA publication the maintainers checked. Confirm them before relying
      on the numbers.
    </div>

    <h3>The BC tax reduction</h3>
    <p>British Columbia has a low-income credit worth up to
    {{$provinces.BC.tax_reduction.max_credit}} that phases out at
    {{%provinces.BC.tax_reduction.phaseout_rate}} of income above
    {{$provinces.BC.tax_reduction.threshold}}. That adds
    {{%provinces.BC.tax_reduction.phaseout_rate}} to the effective marginal rate of
    everyone in the phase-out band, and it appears in no bracket table anywhere.</p>

    <h3>Why this makes contributions worth more</h3>
    <p>A deduction runs the whole mechanism in reverse. Lowering your taxable income
    returns the tax <b>and</b> restores the benefit you were losing. For a family in a
    clawback band, the benefit restored can exceed the tax refund itself — which is
    exactly what the calculator's "what the government gives back" figure adds up.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">CPP and EI are deliberately excluded</span>
      They are levied on gross employment income and an RRSP deduction does not reduce
      them, so including them would overstate what a contribution is worth. For
      reference, {{PAGE_YEAR}} rates are {{%payroll.cpp.rate}} CPP on earnings between
      {{$payroll.cpp.basic_exemption}} and {{$payroll.cpp.ympe}},
      {{%payroll.cpp2.rate}} on the slice up to {{$payroll.cpp2.yampe}}, and
      {{%payroll.ei.rate}} EI up to {{$payroll.ei.max_insurable}}.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- CREDITS -->
<div class="panel sec" id="credits">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Credits and deductions</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>These two words get used interchangeably and mean different things.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A <b>deduction</b> reduces the income you are taxed on. It is worth your
      marginal rate — a {{$accounts.tfsa.annual_limit}} RRSP contribution saves more
      for a high earner than a low earner.</li>
      <li>A <b>non-refundable credit</b> reduces the tax you owe, calculated at the
      lowest rate — federally {{%federal.credit_rate}}, and
      {{%provinces.BC.credit_rate}} in BC. It is worth the same to everyone, and it
      cannot take your tax below zero.</li>
      <li>A <b>refundable credit</b> is paid to you even if you owe no tax at all.
      The GST/HST credit and the Canada Workers Benefit work this way, which is why
      filing a return matters even on a very low income.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The basic personal amount</h3>
    <p>Everyone gets a slice of income effectively tax-free through a non-refundable
    credit. Federally the amount is {{$federal.bpa.base}}, plus a supplement of up to
    {{$federal.bpa.additional}} that phases out between {{$federal.bpa.phaseout_start}}
    and {{$federal.bpa.phaseout_end}}. British Columbia's is
    {{$provinces.BC.bpa}}.</p>

    <h3>Credits worth knowing about</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Canada Workers Benefit</b> — refundable, for lower-income workers.</li>
      <li><b>GST/HST credit</b> — refundable, paid quarterly, income-tested. Like the
      CCB it phases out, so it adds to the effective marginal rate in its band.</li>
      <li><b>Disability tax credit</b> — substantial, transferable to a supporting
      relative, and a gateway to the Registered Disability Savings Plan.</li>
      <li><b>Medical expenses</b> — claimable above a threshold based on your income,
      and usually best claimed by the lower-income spouse.</li>
      <li><b>Tuition</b> — carried forward or transferred to a parent or grandparent.</li>
      <li><b>Charitable donations, pension income, age amount, caregiver amounts</b> —
      each with its own rules.</li>
      <li><b>Spousal transfers</b> — many credits move to a partner when the first
      person cannot use them.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Dollar amounts deliberately omitted</span>
      Only the figures this project sources and version-controls are quoted with
      numbers on this page. The credits above change most years and several are
      province-specific; check current amounts on canada.ca rather than trusting a
      figure memorised from a blog post.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- PENSIONS -->
<div class="panel sec" id="pensions">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">CPP, OAS and GIS</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Government retirement income covers the first slice of what you need, so your
    savings only have to cover the rest. Calculators that ignore it make the target
    look terrifying; calculators that assume the maximum make it look too easy.</p>
    <h3>CPP — Canada Pension Plan</h3>
    <p>A pension you build by working and contributing. The <b>maximum</b> at 65
    assumes roughly 39 years of contributions at the earnings ceiling, which is why the
    average new pension is far below it — the calculator uses the average deliberately.
    Starting before 65 permanently reduces it; starting later permanently increases it.
    CPP is taxable income.</p>
    <h3>OAS — Old Age Security</h3>
    <p>Paid from 65 based on how long you have lived in Canada, not on whether you
    worked. Full residency is 40 years after 18; less is prorated. Payments increase at
    75. OAS is taxable and is clawed back above an income threshold — one of the few
    places where a high retirement income genuinely reduces a benefit.</p>
    <h3>GIS — Guaranteed Income Supplement</h3>
    <p>A non-taxable top-up for low-income seniors receiving OAS, reduced sharply as
    other income rises — roughly 50 cents per dollar. Because RRSP and RRIF withdrawals
    count as income and <b>TFSA withdrawals do not</b>, the choice between those two
    accounts matters enormously for anyone likely to receive GIS.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- BUDGET -->
<div class="panel sec" id="budget">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Budgeting</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>The calculator asks what you can save each month. Working that out honestly is
    the hardest input on the page, and the one with the most leverage.</p>
    <h3>Start from what actually left your account</h3>
    <p>Not what you think you spend — three months of statements. Almost everyone
    discovers a subscription they forgot and a category twice the size they assumed.</p>
    <h3>Three buckets</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Fixed</b> — rent or mortgage, insurance, utilities, transit, debt
      minimums. Hard to change quickly, and the biggest wins hide here.</li>
      <li><b>Variable</b> — groceries, fuel, everything discretionary. Easy to change,
      usually smaller than people expect.</li>
      <li><b>Savings</b> — treated as a bill you pay yourself, not as whatever is left
      over. Nothing is ever left over.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A common starting split is roughly half your take-home to needs, thirty percent
    to wants, twenty percent to savings and debt. Treat it as a sanity check, not a
    rule — it is unreachable in an expensive housing market, and unambitious on a high
    income.</p>
    <h3>Automate it</h3>
    <p>Move savings the day you are paid. A transfer that happens automatically
    survives a bad week; one that depends on willpower at month-end does not. Increase
    the amount whenever your pay rises, before you adjust to the higher income.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Irregular income</span>
      If you are self-employed or on variable hours, budget from your <em>lowest</em>
      recent month and treat anything above it as a windfall to allocate deliberately.
      Also set aside tax and CPP as the money arrives — nobody withholds it for you.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- EMERGENCY -->
<div class="panel sec" id="emergency">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">The emergency fund</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Cash you can reach today, for the things that actually happen: a job loss, a
    car repair, a flight home. Its job is not to earn — it is to stop a bad month
    becoming credit card debt that takes years to clear.</p>
    <h3>How much</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Starter:</b> one month of essential spending, or around a thousand
      dollars. Enough to absorb an ordinary shock.</li>
      <li><b>Full:</b> three to six months of <em>essential</em> spending — the fixed
      bucket, not your whole lifestyle.</li>
      <li><b>More</b> if your income is variable, you are the only earner, or you work
      in a field where finding the next job takes months.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Where to keep it</h3>
    <p>Somewhere boring and immediately accessible: a high-interest savings account, or
    a TFSA holding cash or a money-market fund. This is the one case where cash in a
    TFSA is the right answer. Do not put it in stocks — the moment you need it is
    disproportionately likely to be a moment when markets are also down.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">Using a TFSA for this</span>
      Withdrawing restores your room, but not until January 1 of the following year.
      If you empty it in March and refill it in November, you have over-contributed and
      will be charged 1% a month on the excess.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- DEBT -->
<div class="panel sec" id="debt">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Debt</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Paying off debt is an investment with a guaranteed, tax-free return equal to its
    interest rate. Compare that rate against what you could reasonably expect to earn —
    the calculator defaults to 8% a year, before tax, and with no guarantee whatsoever.
    A credit card at 20% wins that comparison every time.</p>
    <h3>A rough ranking</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Above ~10%</b> — payday loans, credit cards, most store cards. Clear these
      before investing anything beyond an employer match.</li>
      <li><b>Roughly 5–10%</b> — car loans, unsecured lines of credit, some student
      debt. A close call. Splitting between the debt and investing is defensible.</li>
      <li><b>Below ~5%</b> — most mortgages, subsidised student loans. Paying the
      minimum and investing the difference is usually the better expected outcome,
      though paying down debt is the certain one.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Two ways to attack multiple debts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Highest rate first (avalanche)</b> — mathematically optimal; costs the
      least in total interest.</li>
      <li><b>Smallest balance first (snowball)</b> — costs slightly more, but clears
      individual debts sooner. The method you actually stick with beats the one that is
      theoretically better.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Canadian specifics</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Interest on student loans may qualify for a non-refundable credit; interest
      on consumer debt does not.</li>
      <li>Interest on money borrowed to earn investment income can be deductible, with
      strict tracing rules. Get advice before relying on that.</li>
      <li>Consolidating debt only helps if the new rate is genuinely lower <em>and</em>
      the freed-up room does not get spent again.</li>
      <li>A mortgage is not an emergency fund. Money paid into it is difficult to get
      back at exactly the moment you would need it most.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- GROWTH -->
<div class="panel sec" id="growth">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Compound growth</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Growth earns growth. That is the entire idea, and it is why the calculator's
    chart bends upward instead of running straight.</p>
    <p>The chart splits the balance into what you contributed, the government help
    reinvested, and the growth on both. Early on, almost all of it is your own money —
    which is why the first years feel like nothing is happening. The point where the
    growth band overtakes everything you have put in is marked on the chart, and after
    it your investments are contributing more each year than you are.</p>
    <p>This is why starting earlier beats saving harder later: a dollar invested at 25
    has forty years to compound, and a dollar invested at 55 has ten. The calculator's
    "cost of waiting" figure is that difference, and it is always larger than people
    expect, because the years you skip are the ones with the most compounding left.</p>
    <div class="callout warnbox">
      <span class="t">A flat rate is a simplification</span>
      Real returns arrive unevenly. A 30-year average of 8% can contain several years
      of losses, and the order in which good and bad years arrive matters a great deal
      near retirement. This model uses one steady rate and no market crashes.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- DOLLARS -->
<div class="panel sec" id="dollars">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">Future dollars</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>The calculator reports balances in <b>future dollars</b> — the actual number
    that would appear on a statement, with no inflation adjustment.</p>
    <p>Keep in mind what that means. Prices rise, so a sum decades away will not buy
    what the same sum buys now. At 2% inflation, purchasing power roughly halves over
    35 years. A seven-figure balance in forty years is a real number, but it is not a
    forty-year-from-now fortune in today's terms.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="t">One comparison to treat carefully</span>
      In <em>Are you on track?</em>, CPP and OAS are shown at today's payment rates
      while your projected portfolio income is in future dollars. Both pensions are
      indexed in reality, so the two sides of that comparison sit on different
      footings. Read the split between them as indicative rather than exact.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- LIMITS -->
<div class="panel sec" id="limits">
  <div class="panel-head"><span class="eyebrow">What this does not model</span></div>
  <div class="panel-body ltext">
    <p>Being clear about the edges is part of being trustworthy about the middle.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>British Columbia only.</b> Other provinces have their own brackets,
      credits and child benefits. Quebec is a separate system entirely.</li>
      <li><b>BCFB and CGEB parameters are not independently verified.</b> They are
      modelled, and recorded in the tax-data file as user-supplied. Confirm them against
      CRA and BC publications before relying on the output.</li>
      <li><b>GIS and the OAS clawback</b> are flagged by the guardrails but not
      modelled, so retirement-age scenarios are incomplete.</li>
      <li><b>One flat rate of return</b>, no market volatility, no sequence-of-returns
      risk.</li>
      <li><b>Contribution room is estimated</b> from the income you type. Your real
      room is in CRA My Account.</li>
      <li><b>No self-employment, rental, capital-gains or dividend income</b>, and none
      of the credits listed above beyond the basic personal amounts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>All rates come from <span class="num">data/taxyear_{{PAGE_YEAR}}.json</span>, and
    every figure on this page is substituted from that file when the site is built, so
    the explanations cannot drift away from the engine.</p>
    <div class="callout warnbox">
      <span class="t">Not advice</span>
      This is an educational model. It is not tax, financial, or investment advice, and
      it does not account for every credit, deduction, or personal circumstance.
      Confirm your own contribution room in CRA My Account, and consider speaking to a
      qualified professional before acting. "Financial Planner" and "Financial Adviser"
      are protected titles in Ontario and Saskatchewan; this project is a calculator and
      a model, and describes itself as nothing more.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

</div>

<footer>
  Federal brackets: CRA {{PAGE_YEAR}}. British Columbia brackets: Province of B.C. Canada Child Benefit: 2026–27 benefit year.
  CPP and OAS: Canada.ca, January 2026 — average, not maximum. Balances are shown in future dollars, without an inflation adjustment.
  Everything runs in your browser; nothing you type is sent anywhere or stored. The only thing kept on
  your device is whether you prefer the light or dark theme.
</footer>
